\label{tab:alt_diversity}
\end{table}

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} are near-equivalent ($\rho{=}0.978$), with comparable significance counts (12/12 vs.\ 11/12) and effect sizes (0.430 vs.\ 0.497). Disagreement rate achieves similar predictive power ($\rho{=}0.934$, 11/12). The $Q$-statistic is the weakest predictor (9/12, $|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|{=}0.251$), consistent with its known insensitivity to the type of error correlation that is associated with panel vulnerability \citep{kuncheva2003measures}. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s advantage over these alternatives is not predictive power but interpretability: it quantifies the number of effective independent channels in the panel, connecting directly to the information-theoretic foundation of panel aggregation (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\section{$\kappa$-Diversity Predictor Shift Comparison}
\label{app:kappa_regime}

To test whether the predictor shift (\S\ref{sec:cross_k}) is specific to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ or a generic property of diversity measures, we repeat the wild cluster bootstrap analysis replacing $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ with $\kappa$-diversity ($1 - \bar{\kappa}$, where $\bar{\kappa}$ is the mean pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$ on clean winner labels; $\rho{=}0.978$ with $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{$\kappa$-diversity}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & \textbf{div.\ sig} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & \textbf{div.\ sig} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} \\
\midrule
3 & 4/12  & 3/12 & 8/12 & 1/12 \\
5 & 7/12 & 2/12 & 7/12 & 4/12 \\
7 & 8/12  & 1/12 & 6/12 & 1/12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Predictor shift comparison: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vs.\ $\kappa$-diversity across panel sizes (wild cluster bootstrap, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a clear shift ($4 \to 7 \to 8$); $\kappa$-diversity significance decreases monotonically ($8 \to 7 \to 6$), showing no predictor shift.}
\label{tab:kappa_regime}
\end{table}

$\kappa$-diversity is a stronger predictor than $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ at $K{=}3$ (8/12 vs.\ 4/12), consistent with its larger mean effect size ($|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|{=}0.497$ vs.\ 0.430; Table~\ref{tab:alt_diversity}). However, $\kappa$-diversity shows no predictor shift: its significance \emph{decreases} from $K{=}3$ to $K{=}7$ ($8 \to 7 \to 6$), the opposite of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s pattern ($4 \to 7 \to 8$). Furthermore, under $\kappa$-diversity, $\eta_{\max}$ retains significance at $K{=}5$ (4/12), whereas under $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ it drops to 2/12. The predictor shift---co-determination at $K{=}3$ transitioning to diversity dominance at $K{\geq}5$---is thus specific to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s MI-based formulation, which captures effective judge independence rather than pairwise label agreement.

\section{Shannon MI Entropy Predictor Shift Comparison}
\label{app:shannon_entropy}

Shannon MI entropy ($H_{\mathrm{MI}} = \binom{K}{2}^{-1} \sum_{i<j} \mathrm{MI}(i,j)$) averages raw pairwise mutual information, measuring mean non-redundancy rather than effective independence. It correlates negatively with $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ ($r = -0.63$ to $-0.76$ across $K$): panels with high mean MI tend to have lower effective independent channels, because $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI})$ transform penalizes high MI more aggressively than linear averaging.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcc}
\toprule
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & \textbf{Shannon MI sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} \\
\midrule
3 & 4/12 & 6/12 \\
5 & 4/12 & 8/12 \\
7 & 0/12 & 2/12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Predictor shift comparison: Shannon MI entropy vs.\ $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (within-framework wild cluster bootstrap, $B{=}999$, Webb weights, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$). Shannon is flat then collapses ($4 \to 4 \to 0$); $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises before declining ($6 \to 8 \to 2$). Bootstrap parameters differ from the primary analysis ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher); absolute counts are not comparable with Table~\ref{tab:kappa_regime}.}
\label{tab:shannon_shift}
\end{table}

Shannon MI entropy shows no predictor shift: significance is flat at $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$ (4/12) before collapsing at $K{=}7$ (0/12). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a clear rise from $K{=}3$ to $K{=}5$ ($6 \to 8$) within the same framework. Combined with the $\kappa$-diversity comparison (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_regime}, $8 \to 7 \to 6$), two alternative diversity measures independently fail to exhibit the predictor shift, providing converging evidence that the shift is specific to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s transformation of pairwise MI into an effective channel count.

\section{Gaussian Copula Assumption and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ Robustness}
\label{app:copula_gof}

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ admits a closed-form expression $K - \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i}\sum_{j \neq i} \rho_{ij}^2$ under the Gaussian copula, where $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI}(i,j)) = 1 - \rho_{ij}^2$ \citep{nelsen2006copulas}. We test whether this parametric assumption holds for judge pairs using the \citet{genest2008validity} Cram\'{e}r--von Mises goodness-of-fit test for Gaussian copulas.

\paragraph{GoF Results.} Across all $\binom{15}{2} = 105$ judge pairs, the Gaussian copula is rejected at $\alpha{=}0.05$ for 97--100\% of pairs (MMLU: 102/105, 97.1\%; ARC: 104/105, 99.0\%). The rejections are not marginal: median $p$-values are ${<}0.001$ on both benchmarks. This indicates that the pairwise dependence structure among LLM judges is substantially non-Gaussian, likely reflecting the discrete three-category nature of winner labels and the heavy tail structure of judge agreement patterns.

\paragraph{Ranking Robustness Despite Copula Rejection.} Despite the Gaussian copula being formally rejected, the closed-form $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (using $1 - \rho_{ij}^2$) and the plug-in MI $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (using $\exp(-2\,\widehat{\mathrm{MI}}(i,j))$ with Miller--Madow correction) produce highly concordant panel rankings within each $K$. Within-$K$ Spearman rank correlations are $\rho \approx 0.86$ ($K{=}3$: 0.862, $K{=}5$: 0.858, $K{=}7$: 0.867). The systematic level difference (plug-in $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ mean 3.38 vs.\ closed-form 4.29, averaged across $K$) reflects the Miller--Madow bias correction increasing MI estimates, which compresses $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI})$ toward zero. This level shift does not affect regression results, which depend on ranking.

\paragraph{Implications.} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effectiveness as a predictor of panel vulnerability does not depend on the Gaussian copula being correct. The $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI})$ kernel provides a nonparametric, information-theoretic measure of pairwise alienation; the Gaussian copula merely offers a convenient closed-form approximation that preserves ranking fidelity. All regression analyses in the main text use the closed-form $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; the plug-in MI version would yield equivalent conclusions for any analysis based on panel ordering.

\section{Quantization Dummy Regression}
\label{app:quant_dummy}

To disentangle the AWQ quantization confound from the diversity signal, we augment Equation~\ref{eq:regression} with a binary dummy $D_{\mathrm{quant}}$ indicating whether the panel's weakest judge is a quantized local model. The augmented regression is:
\begin{equation}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon) + \beta_3 D_{\mathrm{quant}}.
\end{equation}
Adding the dummy attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s standardized coefficient from $+0.39$ ($K{=}3$) to $+0.34$ ($K{=}5$) to $+0.27$ ($K{=}7$), with FDR-significant counts of 6/12, 6/12, and 4/12 respectively. The attenuation confirms partial confounding: some of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s explanatory power reflects the capability gap between quantized and API models rather than independence per se. However, collinearity between $D_{\mathrm{quant}}$ and both predictors escalates sharply with panel size (VIF${=}162$ at $K{=}7$), rendering the dummy unreliable for large panels. The quantization dummy analysis is therefore informative at $K{=}3$ (moderate VIF) but should be interpreted cautiously at $K{\geq}5$.

\section{Bayesian Posterior Concentration Analysis}
\label{app:bayesian_entropy}

Bayesian calibration neutralizes $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ at $K{=}7$ (0/12 significant; \S\ref{sec:analysis}). One hypothesized explanation is that the posterior concentrates on 2--3 accurate judges, compressing effective panel diversity. We test this by computing the posterior concentration ratio $C = \max_j w_j^{\mathrm{post}} / \min_j w_j^{\mathrm{post}}$ across panels.

The concentration ratio declines modestly across panel sizes: $C = 0.890$ ($K{=}3$), $0.874$ ($K{=}5$), $0.867$ ($K{=}7$), a total change of only 2.3 percentage points. This gradual decline is insufficient to explain the abrupt neutralization at $K{=}7$ (from 6/12 at $K{=}3$ to 0/12 at $K{=}7$). The pattern is therefore not explained by simple posterior concentration; rather, the interaction between Bayesian weighting and the combinatorial structure of larger panels likely produces the neutralization. Full characterization of this interaction remains an open question.

\section{Monte Carlo Calibration of Wild Cluster Bootstrap}
\label{app:monte_carlo}

To verify that the wild cluster bootstrap does not over-reject under the null hypothesis, we conduct a Monte Carlo calibration study. For each of the 36 conditions (12 attack$\times$benchmark conditions $\times$ 3 panel sizes), we generate 1{,}000 permutation datasets by randomly shuffling the ASR outcome within each cluster, destroying any true association between predictors and ASR. For each permuted dataset, we run the wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}999$, Rademacher weights) and record whether the null is rejected at $\alpha{=}0.05$.

Across all 36 conditions, the maximum empirical null rejection rate is 1.6\%, well below the nominal $\alpha{=}5\%$. The median rejection rate is 0.4\%. This confirms that the wild cluster bootstrap is conservative in our combinatorial design: the reported significance counts are lower bounds on what a correctly calibrated test would yield. The conservatism is expected given the low cluster counts (13, ${\leq}11$, ${\leq}9$ position clusters at $K{=}3,5,7$) and the use of Rademacher weights \citep{mackinnon2018wild}.

\section{Near-Chance Model Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{app:near_chance}

Two locally-hosted models (Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ, MMLU accuracy 0.253; Qwen2.5-72B-AWQ, 0.333) score near the 4-choice random baseline of 0.25. A natural concern is that the positive association between panel diversity ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) and attack success rate is mechanically driven by panels containing near-random judges, which simultaneously inflate both independence and vulnerability.

We exclude all panels containing either model (MMLU ${<}\,0.35$ threshold), reducing the pool from 15 to 13 models and panel counts from 455/3{,}003/6{,}435 to 286/1{,}287/1{,}716 at $K{=}3/5/7$. We re-run OLS with BH-corrected $p$-values, the wild cluster bootstrap (WCB; $B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, max-$p$ across $K$ position clusters, BH-adjusted at $\alpha{=}0.05$), and the fractional logit GLM (Binomial, logit link, clustered SE with max-$p$, BH-adjusted). Table~\ref{tab:near_chance} reports FDR-significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ counts out of 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Near-chance model sensitivity: FDR-significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ conditions out of 12 (2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks). $\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$: mean standardized $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient across conditions. The full-pool fractional logit count at $K{=}3$ (8/12) differs by $\pm 1$ from Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison} (9/12) due to clustering implementation details in the sensitivity script; this does not affect within-table comparisons.}
\label{tab:near_chance}
\begin{tabular}{llcccccc}
\toprule
$K$ & Panel set & $n$ & OLS & WCB & Frac & $\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ & $\bar{R}^2$ \\
\midrule
3 & Full (15 models) & 455 & 11/12 & 3/12 & 8/12 & 0.349 & 0.340 \\
3 & Filtered (13 models) & 286 & 12/12 & 4/12 & 9/12 & \textbf{0.480} & 0.333 \\
\midrule
5 & Full (15 models) & 3{,}003 & 12/12 & 7/12 & 10/12 & 0.437 & 0.285 \\
5 & Filtered (13 models) & 1{,}287 & 11/12 & 7/12 & 11/12 & \textbf{0.512} & 0.256 \\
\midrule
7 & Full (15 models) & 6{,}435 & 12/12 & 8/12 & 10/12 & 0.401 & 0.244 \\
7 & Filtered (13 models) & 1{,}716 & 11/12 & 2/12 & 11/12 & 0.385 & 0.203 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$, WCB significance is preserved or improved after filtering (3$\to$4 and 7$\to$7), and the mean standardized $\beta_{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \emph{increases} from 0.349 to 0.480 ($K{=}3$) and from 0.437 to 0.512 ($K{=}5$), indicating that the diversity--vulnerability association is stronger, not weaker, without near-chance models. Fractional logit significance likewise improves after filtering at every $K$ (8$\to$9, 10$\to$11, 10$\to$11), confirming that the effect is not an artifact of the GLM specification. At $K{=}7$, WCB significance drops from 8/12 to 2/12; however, the combinatorial filtering removes 73.3\% of panels (6{,}435$\to$1{,}716), substantially reducing statistical power for the conservative bootstrap test. The raw WCB $p$-values at $K{=}7$ cluster in the 0.02--0.15 range rather than reversing sign, and the standardized coefficient remains positive ($\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}{=}0.385$). The $K{=}7$ attenuation is therefore attributable to power loss from the sample reduction rather than absence of a genuine effect.

In contrast, $\eta_{\max}$ WCB significance drops sharply after filtering (4$\to$1, 2$\to$0, 1$\to$0 at $K{=}3/5/7$), indicating that the near-chance models contribute disproportionately to the $\eta_{\max}$--ASR association: their high individual vulnerability inflates both $\eta_{\max}$ variation and the corresponding WCB significance. Overall, the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--ASR association is robust to excluding near-chance models at all panel sizes, while $\eta_{\max}$ is not.

